From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jaime Dias <jaimejosediasnt@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 7 Aug 2026 00:30:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 9/9] dos: do not serve ELF loads from an unfilled buffer

elf_read_block() caches small reads in a 4 KB buffer. It fills that buffer
with a 4096-byte read that is allowed to come up short -- near end of file it
always does -- and then discards the result. The buffer comes from
AllocMem(MEMF_ANY) and is never cleared, and the cache-hit test bounds
requests by LOADSEG_SMALL_READ rather than by how much was actually read. A
request reaching past the filled part is served from uninitialised memory,
handed to the ELF loader as file contents, with no error raised anywhere.

Not hypothetical. Tracing every read of an AROS boot on m68k-emu68 found it
happening twice per boot, at the same offsets in all three runs traced:

  off=7412  size=400  buffer filled from 5688, 1904 valid ->  220 bytes past
  off=32292 size=520  buffer filled from 30344, 1172 valid -> 1296 bytes past

The port crashes inside InternalLoadSeg_ELF with the guest PC in a
zero-filled region of a freshly loaded segment. See
AI_context/issues/ISSUE-0007.md.

The fix records how much the fill actually delivered and bounds the cache by
that. A short fill stays what it is -- normal at end of file, not an error --
and only limits what may be served. A request the file genuinely cannot
satisfy now reports ERROR_BAD_HUNK instead of inventing the bytes.

The recursive re-read is gone with it: the fill starts at the requested
offset, so the request is at the head of the buffer and can be copied
directly. That also removes the possibility of recursing forever when the
refill cannot satisfy the request either.

Upstream defect, latent wherever the filesystem happens not to read short.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
--- a/rom/dos/internalloadseg_elf.c
+++ b/rom/dos/internalloadseg_elf.c
@@ -52,6 +52,7 @@
 {
     ULONG   srb_FileOffset;
     APTR    srb_Buffer;
+    ULONG   srb_Valid;      /* bytes actually read into srb_Buffer */
 };
 
 static int elf_read_block
@@ -67,8 +68,22 @@
 {
     D(bug("[ELF Loader] elf_read_block (offset=%d, size=%d)\n", offset, size));
 
+    /*
+     * srb_Valid, not LOADSEG_SMALL_READ.
+     *
+     * The buffer is filled with a 4 KB read that is allowed to come up short --
+     * near end of file it always does -- and the fill's result was discarded.
+     * srb_Buffer comes from AllocMem(MEMF_ANY) and is never cleared, so serving
+     * a request that reaches past what was actually read hands the loader
+     * whatever was in that memory, as file contents, with no error anywhere.
+     *
+     * Not hypothetical: tracing every read of an AROS boot on m68k found it
+     * happening twice per boot at fixed offsets, delivering 220 and 1296 bytes
+     * of uninitialised memory into loaded modules. See
+     * AI_context/issues/ISSUE-0007.md.
+     */
     if (size <= LOADSEG_SMALL_READ && (offset >= srb->srb_FileOffset) &&
-            ((offset + size) <= (srb->srb_FileOffset + LOADSEG_SMALL_READ)) &&
+            ((offset + size) <= (srb->srb_FileOffset + srb->srb_Valid)) &&
             srb->srb_Buffer != NULL)
     {
         CopyMem(srb->srb_Buffer + (offset - srb->srb_FileOffset), buffer, size);
@@ -83,13 +98,40 @@
         {
             if (srb->srb_Buffer == NULL)
                 srb->srb_Buffer = AllocMem(LOADSEG_SMALL_READ, MEMF_ANY);
+            if (srb->srb_Buffer == NULL)
+                return read_block(file, buffer, size, funcarray, DOSBase);
 
+            /* Fill the buffer, recording how much actually arrived. A short
+             * read here is normal at end of file and is not an error; it only
+             * bounds what the cache may serve. */
             srb->srb_FileOffset = offset;
+            srb->srb_Valid = 0;
+            while (srb->srb_Valid < LOADSEG_SMALL_READ)
+            {
+                LONG got = ilsRead(file, srb->srb_Buffer + srb->srb_Valid,
+                    LOADSEG_SMALL_READ - srb->srb_Valid);
+
+                if (got <= 0)
+                    break;
+
+                srb->srb_Valid += got;
+            }
+
+            if (size > srb->srb_Valid)
+            {
+                /* The file really does not have that many bytes here. Report it
+                 * the way read_block would rather than inventing them. */
+                if (DOSBase)
+                {
+                    struct Process *me = (struct Process *)FindTask(NULL);
+                    me->pr_Result2 = ERROR_BAD_HUNK;
+                }
+                return 1;
+            }
 
-            /* Fill the buffer */
-            read_block(file, srb->srb_Buffer, LOADSEG_SMALL_READ, funcarray, DOSBase);
-            /* Re-read, now srb will be used */
-            return elf_read_block(file, offset, buffer, size, funcarray, srb, DOSBase);
+            /* The fill started at `offset`, so the request is at its head. */
+            CopyMem(srb->srb_Buffer, buffer, size);
+            return 0;
         }
         else
         {
